.TH LIBESDM 3 "2026-08-05" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
libesdm \- Entropy Source and DRNG Manager library
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <esdm/esdm.h>
.B #include <esdm/esdm_config.h>
.PP
.B pkg\-config \-\-cflags \-\-libs esdm
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libesdm
is the ESDM itself: it manages the entropy sources, maintains the
deterministic random number generators seeded from them, and hands out random
data. Linking against it embeds a complete ESDM instance into the calling
process.
.PP
This is not what most applications want. An application that needs random data
from the system's ESDM should talk to
.BR esdm\-server (8)
through
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3)
instead, so that it shares one entropy accounting with the rest of the system.
.B libesdm
is for building a daemon like
.BR esdm\-server (8)
itself, or for a self\-contained application that deliberately runs its own
ESDM.
.PP
Symbols are versioned. The supported API described here is exported under the
version node
.BR LIBESDM_1.0 .
Symbols carrying the
.B LIBESDM_PRIVATE_1.0
node exist for the programs shipped with the ESDM and are not API.
.SH LIFE CYCLE
.TP
.BI "int esdm_init(void);"
Initialize the library: configuration, DRNG manager, entropy sources and the
status shared memory segment. Must be called before any other function.
Returns 0 on success and a negative errno value on error. The ESDM is
initialized once per process: after
.BR esdm_fini (),
or after an
.BR esdm_init ()
that failed and tore down what it had set up, the call fails with
.BR \-ESHUTDOWN .
.TP
.BI "int esdm_reinit(void);"
Reinitialize configuration, DRNG manager and entropy sources without losing
state or entropy \- for instance after a configuration change. Must not run
concurrently with
.BR esdm_init ().
Like
.BR esdm_init (),
it fails with
.B \-ESHUTDOWN
once the ESDM was torn down.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_init_monitor(void (*" priv_init_completion ")(void));"
Run the entropy source monitor. The monitor watches the entropy sources for
newly arriving entropy while not all DRNGs are seeded yet, and it is what
performs the periodic collection from the slow entropy sources afterwards.
The call blocks until the ESDM is finalized, so it is normally run on a thread
of its own; running it in the calling thread is a way to synchronously wait
for all DRNGs to be initialized. The optional
.I priv_init_completion
callback is invoked once the part of the initialization that requires
privileges has completed, which is the point at which a daemon may drop them.
.TP
.BI "void esdm_fini(void);"
Terminate the entropy sources and the DRNG manager and release all resources.
This also stops a monitor started with
.BR esdm_init_monitor ().
The ESDM cannot be initialized again afterwards.
.SH FORK
A process that calls
.BR fork (2)
after
.BR esdm_init ()
hands its ESDM down to the child, the states of all DRNGs included. Before a
DRNG produces its first output in the child, or takes its next seed there, it
is reseeded with data unique to the child \- 32 bytes of the kernel's random
number generator, the process ID and the time \- and a reseed from the entropy
sources is forced to follow by the usual rules: the child repeats neither the
random numbers of its parent nor those of another child.
.PP
The threads of the ESDM are not inherited: the child has no reseed worker, no
entropy source monitor and no periodic self tests, and the reseed worker is not
started again in it. Its DRNGs are reseeded by the requests that run into their
reseed condition.
As with any library using threads, a lock that another thread of the parent
held at the moment of the fork stays taken in the child \- one of the ESDM's
own threads among them, such as the reseed worker while it reseeds a DRNG \-
and a request of the child that needs it waits forever. A child that is to
rely on the ESDM is better off with
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3),
or with an ESDM of its own, initialized after the fork. A child leaves with
.BR _exit (2)
or
.BR execve (2),
not with
.BR esdm_fini (),
which waits for the threads of the parent and tears down the status shared
memory segment the parent still serves.
.SH OBTAINING RANDOM DATA
.TP
.BI "ssize_t esdm_get_random_bytes(uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " nbytes ");"
Random data without a guarantee that the ESDM is fully seeded.
.TP
.BI "ssize_t esdm_get_random_bytes_full(uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " nbytes ");"
Random data, blocking until the ESDM is fully seeded and until all
.I nbytes
are generated \- also while the DRNGs reached the most they may generate
without a full reseed and wait for it, which in a build capping that output
(SP800\-90C, FIPS 140, AIS 20/31 DRG.4) sustained load makes them do all the
time. Fewer bytes, or an error, are returned only after an error that no wait
cures, such as a failed self test.
.TP
.BI "ssize_t esdm_get_random_bytes_full_noblock(uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " nbytes ");"
The same, but returning an error instead of blocking:
.B \-EAGAIN
while the ESDM is not operational. It may also return fewer bytes than asked
for, when a DRNG reached that limit on the way. When every DRNG the request
could go to is at that limit, it is served by the initial DRNG after its
reseed, for which it waits.
.TP
.BI "ssize_t esdm_get_random_bytes_pr(uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " nbytes ");"
Random data with prediction resistance: the DRNG is reseeded from the entropy
sources for this request.
.TP
.BI "ssize_t esdm_get_random_bytes_pr_noblock(uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " nbytes ");"
The same, but returning an error instead of blocking.
.TP
.BI "ssize_t esdm_get_seed(uint64_t *" buf ", size_t " nbytes ", enum esdm_get_seed_flags " flags ");"
Fill
.I buf
with seed material taken directly from the entropy sources, for callers that
want to seed a DRNG of their own rather than use the ESDM's. The buffer
receives the seed length, the entropy rate in bits and the seed data itself.
If the buffer is too small,
.B \-EINVAL
or
.B \-EMSGSIZE
is returned, the latter with the required length stored in the buffer.
.I flags
is a combination of
.B ESDM_GET_SEED_NONBLOCK
(return
.B \-EAGAIN
instead of blocking) and
.B ESDM_GET_SEED_FULLY_SEEDED
(the caller's DRNG is already seeded, so the smaller runtime entropy rate
suffices instead of the initial seeding rate).
.PP
All of these return the number of bytes written, or a negative errno value.
.SH STATUS
.TP
.BI "int esdm_status(char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
Status of the ESDM and its entropy sources as human readable text: the JSON
document of
.BR esdm_status_json ()
printed with a line "Label: value" per member and a section, indented one
space deeper, per object \- one per entropy source and DRNG instance.
Returns 0 on success and
.B \-EMSGSIZE
if the report does not fit into the buffer; what fits is left in it,
NUL\-terminated.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_status_json(char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
The same information as a JSON document. Returns 0 on success and
.B \-EMSGSIZE
if the document does not fit into the buffer. A document is never cut short:
a consumer could not tell half a JSON document from a whole one, so the buffer
then holds the empty string.
.TP
.BI "void esdm_status_machine(struct esdm_status_st *" status ");"
The same information in a structure meant for programmatic use.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_status_drng_json(uint32_t " node ", char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
The state of the DRNG instance of
.I node
as a JSON document. Returns 0 on success,
.B \-ENODEV
if the node has no DRNG instance, which tells a caller walking the nodes
from 0 upwards that it reached the end, and
.B \-EMSGSIZE
if the document does not fit into the buffer, which then holds the empty
string as for
.BR esdm_status_json ().
.TP
.BI "int esdm_status_drng_pr_json(char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
The same for the DRNG instance serving prediction resistance requests. That
DRNG is seeded anew for every request, so its counters and seeding times
would tell every local user how much prediction resistant data others asked
for, and when:
.IR seconds_until_reseed ,
.IR requests_since_fully_seeded ,
.IR generated_bits_since_fully_seeded ,
.IR seed_generation ,
.I last_seeding_time
and
.I seconds_since_last_reseed
are null for it, here and in the status of
.BR esdm_status_json ()
and
.BR esdm_status ().
.TP
.BI "void esdm_version(char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
Version information.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_state_operational(void);"
Whether the ESDM is operational.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_state_fully_seeded(void);"
Whether the ESDM is fully seeded.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_sp80090c_compliant(void);"
Whether the current configuration meets SP800\-90C.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_ntg1_compliant(void);"
Whether it meets AIS 20/31 NTG.1.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_ntg1_2024_compliant(void);"
Whether it meets NTG.1 as of the 2024 revision.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_oversampling(void);"
Whether the SP800\-90C oversampling of the entropy sources is applied. A
build to the AIS 20/31 DRG.3 class applies it without claiming SP800\-90C.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_drg3_compliant(void);"
Whether the build satisfies the AIS 20/31 (2024) DRG.3 class, which a DRG.4
build does as well.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_drg4_compliant(void);"
Whether the build is configured to the AIS 20/31 (2024) DRG.4 class.
.SH ENTROPY POOL
.TP
.BI "int esdm_pool_insert_aux(const uint8_t *" inbuf ", size_t " inbuflen ", uint32_t " entropy_bits ");"
Insert data into the auxiliary pool and credit it with
.I entropy_bits
bits of entropy.
.TP
.BI "uint32_t esdm_avail_entropy(void);"
Entropy currently held by the ESDM, in bits.
.TP
.BI "uint32_t esdm_avail_entropy_aux(void);"
Entropy currently held by the auxiliary pool, in bits.
.TP
.BI "uint32_t esdm_avail_poolsize_aux(void);"
Size of the auxiliary pool, in bits.
.TP
.BI "uint32_t esdm_get_aux_ent(void);"
Entropy accounted for in the auxiliary pool, in bits.
.TP
.BI "uint32_t esdm_get_digestsize(void);"
Size of the state of the XDRBG-256 operating an auxiliary pool, in bits:
the most entropy a pool holds.
.TP
.BI "void esdm_pool_set_entropy(uint32_t " entropy_bits ");"
Set the entropy accounted for in the auxiliary pool.
.TP
.BI "void esdm_drng_force_reseed(void);"
Force a reseed of the DRNGs from the entropy sources.
.TP
.BI "uint32_t esdm_get_write_wakeup_bits(void);"
Entropy level below which writers polling the random device are woken.
.TP
.BI "void esdm_set_write_wakeup_bits(uint32_t " val ");"
Set that level.
.TP
.BI "uint32_t esdm_get_reseed_max_time(void);"
Maximum time in seconds between two reseed operations.
.TP
.BI "void esdm_set_reseed_max_time(uint32_t " seconds ");"
Set that time. Zero seconds reseeds before every request, one hour is the
maximum accepted.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I esdm_config.h
declares the settings that may be queried and changed at runtime, in
particular the per\-entropy\-source entropy rates
.RB ( esdm_config_es_ *_entropy_rate ()
and their
.BR _set ()
counterparts), the number of DRNG nodes
.RB ( esdm_config_max_nodes (),
.BR esdm_config_online_nodes ()),
and the compliance settings
.RB ( esdm_config_fips_enabled (),
.BR esdm_config_sp80090c_compliant (),
.BR esdm_config_force_fips_set ()).
Changing an entropy rate takes effect on the next reseed.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Functions returning
.B int
return 0 on success and a negative errno value on error. Functions returning
.B ssize_t
return the number of bytes produced, or a negative errno value.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <esdm/esdm.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
	uint8_t buf[32];
	ssize_t ret;

	if (esdm_init())
		return 1;

	ret = esdm_get_random_bytes_full(buf, sizeof(buf));
	if (ret != (ssize_t)sizeof(buf)) {
		esdm_fini();
		return 1;
	}

	/* use buf */

	esdm_fini();
	return 0;
}
.fi
.PP
Note that this program blocks in
.BR esdm_get_random_bytes_full ()
until its own ESDM instance is fully seeded, which is exactly the cost that
using
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3)
against a running
.BR esdm\-server (8)
avoids.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3),
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3),
.BR libesdm_aux_client (3),
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR esdm\-tool (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Stephan Mueller <smueller@chronox.de>
